From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zach Taylor <zctaylor.work@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 17:25:45 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 17/30] Match HTTPS egress rules on request path prefixes

Two https rules for one host could not inject different credentials,
because a rule was chosen on hostname and port alone. An https rule now
takes path_prefixes. The rule with the longest matching prefix decides a
request, and a rule with no prefixes covers every other path. The TLS
connection is still allowed when any https rule covers its SNI and port,
since no path exists yet.

A prefix must start and end with "/", so "/zach/" cannot match
"/zachary/x". Compile drops a prefix that does not, so the rule matches
nothing instead of widening. Prefixes pick a credential and are not an
authorization boundary.

Paths are matched raw, up to the query. A path with a dot segment, an
encoded dot, an encoded slash or a backslash is denied when a prefixed
rule covers its name and port, so "/a/../b/" cannot take the catch-all
rule's credential for what the origin reads as "/b/". Admission ties key
on the prefix as well. Across protocols the tie check compares host and
port only.

Fork-Topic: egress-paths
Fork-Upstream: candidate
